1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,850
Labyrinths have existed for well over 4,000 years.

2
00:00:06,850 --> 00:00:16,910
Labyrinths and labyrinthine symbols have been found in regions as diverse as modern-day Turkey, Ireland, Greece, and India.

3
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:19,192
There are various designs of labyrinth

4
00:00:19,192 --> 00:00:23,580
but what they all have in common is a winding spiral path

5
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,040
which leads to a central area.

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:29,060
There is one starting point at the entrance

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:32,250
and the goal is to reach the central area.

8
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:36,599
Finding your way through a labyrinth involves many twists and turns,

9
00:00:36,599 --> 00:00:42,530
but it's not possible to get lost as there is only one single path.

10
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:47,420
In modern times, the word labyrinth has taken on a different meaning

11
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:50,990
and is often used as a synonym for a maze.

12
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:58,280
A maze is quite different as it is a kind of puzzle with an intricate network of paths.

13
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:02,474
Mazes became fashionable in the 15th and 16th centuries in Europe,

14
00:01:02,474 --> 00:01:07,430
and can still be found in the gardens of great houses and palaces.

15
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:10,480
The paths are usually surrounded by thick

16
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:14,010
high hedges so that it's not possible to see over them.

17
00:01:14,010 --> 00:01:20,248
Entering a maze usually involves getting lost a few times before using logic to work out the pattern

18
00:01:20,248 --> 00:01:24,060
and find your way to the centre and then out again.

19
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:29,350
There are lots of dead ends and paths which lead you back to where you started.

20
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:35,640
The word 'maze' is believed to come from a Scandinavian word for a state of confusion.

21
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,830
This is where the word 'amazing' comes from.

22
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:44,980
Labyrinths, on the other hand, have a very different function.

23
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:49,665
Although people now often refer to things they find complicated as labyrinths,

24
00:01:49,665 --> 00:01:53,570
this is not how they were seen in the past.

25
00:01:53,570 --> 00:02:00,880
The winding spiral of the labyrinth has been used for centuries as a metaphor for life's journey.

26
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:04,505
It served as a spiritual reminder that there is purpose

27
00:02:04,505 --> 00:02:09,580
and meaning to our lives and helped to give people a sense of direction.

28
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:13,101
Labyrinths are thought to encourage a feeling of calm

29
00:02:13,101 --> 00:02:15,260
and have been used as a meditation

30
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:19,360
and prayer tool in many cultures over many centuries.

31
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:26,459
The earliest examples of the labyrinth spiral pattern have been found carved into stone,

32
00:02:26,459 --> 00:02:31,530
from Sardinia to Scandinavia, from Arizona to India to Africa.

33
00:02:31,530 --> 00:02:36,610
In Europe, these spiral carvings date from the late Bronze Age.

34
00:02:36,610 --> 00:02:45,210
The Native American Pima tribe wove baskets with a circular labyrinth design that depicted their own cosmology.

35
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:52,170
In Ancient Greece, the labyrinth spiral was used on coins around four thousand years ago.

36
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:57,880
Labyrinths made of mosaics were commonly found in bathhouses, villas

37
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:00,320
and tombs throughout the Roman Empire.

38
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:06,620
In Northern Europe, there were actual physical labyrinths designed for walking on.

39
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:12,010
These were cut into the turf or grass, usually in a circular pattern.

40
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:15,127
The origin of these walking labyrinths remains unclear,

41
00:03:15,127 --> 00:03:18,740
but they were probably used for fertility rites

42
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:20,970
which may date back thousands of years.

43
00:03:20,970 --> 00:03:25,022
Eleven examples of turf labyrinths survive today,

44
00:03:25,022 --> 00:03:28,975
including the largest one at Saffron Walden, England,

45
00:03:29,537 --> 00:03:36,600
which used to have a large tree in the middle of it

46
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:41,190
More recently labyrinths have experienced something of a revival.

47
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:45,102
Some believe that walking a labyrinth promotes healing and mindfulness,

48
00:03:45,102 --> 00:03:49,796
and there are those who believe in its emotional and physical benefits,

49
00:03:49,796 --> 00:03:54,490
which include slower breathing and a restored sense of balance and perspective.

50
00:03:54,490 --> 00:04:01,177
This idea has become so popular that labyrinths have been laid into the floors of spas,

51
00:04:01,177 --> 00:04:04,520
wellness centres and even prisons in recent years.

52
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:15,170
A pamphlet at Colorado Children's Hospital informs patients that 'walking a labyrinth can often calm people in the midst of a crisis'.

53
00:04:15,170 --> 00:04:19,300
And apparently, it's not only patients who benefit.

54
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:25,490
Many visitors find walking a labyrinth less stressful than sitting in a corridor or waiting room.

55
00:04:25,490 --> 00:04:29,970
Some doctors even walk the labyrinth during their breaks.

56
00:04:29,970 --> 00:04:36,500
In some hospitals, patients who can't walk can have a paper 'finger labyrinth' brought to their bed.

57
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:39,778
The science behind the theory is a little sketchy,

58
00:04:39,778 --> 00:04:42,080
but there are dozens of small-scale studies

59
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:45,240
which support claims about the benefits of labyrinths.

60
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:50,903
For example, one study found that walking a labyrinth provided 'short-term calming,

61
00:04:50,903 --> 00:04:55,150
relaxation, and relief from anxiety' for Alzheimer's patients.

62
00:04:55,150 --> 00:05:02,000
So, what is it about labyrinths that makes their appeal so universal? Well...
